I discarded the two hypotheses whose condition is the edited edge's own current value (edit 5 / cond 5 and edit 3 / cond 3). I also discarded edit 4 → conv3 with edge 0 = none, because that condition cuts off the input to the edited edge.

Among the rest, I dropped mirror duplicates and repeats of the same field pair: (4,5) conv/conv, (1,2), the second skip-residualization variant on edge 3, edit 2 conv with cond 3 skip, (3,4) none/none, (5,3) none/none, and (2,1) none/none. Where I had a choice, I kept the hypotheses whose direction is most open to dispute.

{"hypotheses": [{"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Parallel 3x3 convs summed at node 3 are partly substitutable, so the gain from a conv on edge 5 shrinks when edge 4 already supplies one."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "An input-to-output identity is most valuable when it residualizes a deep conv chain starting with a conv on edge 0."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "A strong conv on edge 5 dominates node 3's sum, diluting a pooled edge-4 route and softening the harm of replacing a learnable op there."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Conv capacity already entering node 3 from node 1 makes a direct input-to-output conv partly redundant."}, {"edit_edge": 2, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "When node 2 already receives conv features directly from the input via edge 1, a second conv input via edge 2 offers diminishing returns."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "higher", "why": "An identity shortcut from input to output residualizes the deep conv path through node 2, so a conv on edge 5 trains better and helps more."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Without a direct input-to-output edge, node 1 to node 3 becomes the main shallow route, so adding conv capacity there matters more."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "A skip from node 1 forwards edge-0 conv features straight to the output, so it acts as a learned route rather than a raw input copy."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Without edge 3, node 3 depends on node 1 via edges 4 and 2-5, so emptying node 1 removes most paths to the output."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If edge 0 is none, edge 1 is node 2's only source of input, so cutting it empties node 2 and the edge-5 route entirely."}]}
